1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,724
Last time, we built a baby Instagram and found the big secret: every app is really just three pieces.

2
00:00:06,211 --> 00:00:10,902
A client, a server, and a database, passing a request back and forth.

3
00:00:11,471 --> 00:00:13,131
But I skipped something sneaky.

4
00:00:13,618 --> 00:00:17,520
When your phone sends that request out, how does it even find the right server?

5
00:00:18,182 --> 00:00:19,853
Out of billions of computers?

6
00:00:20,422 --> 00:00:22,280
Tonight we crack those boxes open.

7
00:00:22,768 --> 00:00:27,609
What's actually inside client and server, and how they find and talk to each other.

8
00:00:28,097 --> 00:00:29,374
So let's order some food.

9
00:00:29,943 --> 00:00:30,929
Open Swiggy.

10
00:00:33,000 --> 00:00:35,264
Everything tonight answers just two questions.

11
00:00:35,624 --> 00:00:36,007
One.

12
00:00:36,495 --> 00:00:38,875
What's really inside those words, client and server?

13
00:00:39,362 --> 00:00:41,858
Who does what, and where does your data actually live?

14
00:00:42,520 --> 00:00:43,019
Two.

15
00:00:43,437 --> 00:00:47,478
How do they find each other across the whole internet, and how do they talk once they do?

16
00:00:47,965 --> 00:00:48,848
Two questions.

17
00:00:48,987 --> 00:00:49,765
Let's go.

18
00:00:52,000 --> 00:00:54,740
Here's the one picture to hold in your head all episode.

19
00:00:55,309 --> 00:00:56,145
A restaurant.

20
00:00:57,201 --> 00:00:58,664
The dining room is the client.

21
00:00:59,024 --> 00:01:01,927
It's where you sit, read the menu, and place your order.

22
00:01:02,588 --> 00:01:03,889
Comfortable, friendly.

23
00:01:04,550 --> 00:01:06,489
But no food is actually cooked there.

24
00:01:06,849 --> 00:01:08,161
The kitchen is the server.

25
00:01:08,730 --> 00:01:14,628
That's where the cooking happens, where the recipes live, and where the pantry, your data, is guarded.

26
00:01:15,197 --> 00:01:16,126
You never see it.

27
00:01:16,892 --> 00:01:19,028
And the waiter carrying orders between them?

28
00:01:19,597 --> 00:01:20,747
We'll meet him soon.

29
00:01:23,000 --> 00:01:24,300
So, the client.

30
00:01:24,718 --> 00:01:27,063
That's your phone, running the Swiggy app.

31
00:01:27,551 --> 00:01:28,422
The dining room.

32
00:01:28,909 --> 00:01:31,243
Its whole job is the stuff you see and touch.

33
00:01:31,731 --> 00:01:32,892
It shows you the screen.

34
00:01:33,461 --> 00:01:35,922
The restaurants, the photos, the buttons.

35
00:01:36,410 --> 00:01:38,244
It handles your taps and your typing.

36
00:01:38,603 --> 00:01:40,705
Scrolling, searching, animating.

37
00:01:41,123 --> 00:01:44,292
And when it needs something real, it asks the server.

38
00:01:44,652 --> 00:01:47,439
But notice: it holds no real data of its own.

39
00:01:48,008 --> 00:01:49,795
It's just showing you a copy.

40
00:01:52,000 --> 00:01:52,952
Now the kitchen.

41
00:01:53,219 --> 00:01:53,974
The server.

42
00:01:54,543 --> 00:01:59,488
This is a powerful computer in a data centre, maybe a thousand kilometres away.

43
00:02:00,057 --> 00:02:02,066
It runs the actual logic.

44
00:02:02,368 --> 00:02:03,831
The brains of the whole thing.

45
00:02:04,249 --> 00:02:05,804
It enforces the rules.

46
00:02:06,222 --> 00:02:07,604
What's allowed, what isn't.

47
00:02:07,801 --> 00:02:09,032
And it owns the data.

48
00:02:09,601 --> 00:02:14,848
The database, where every restaurant, every order, every rupee actually lives.

49
00:02:15,615 --> 00:02:16,985
The real source of truth.

50
00:02:19,000 --> 00:02:21,357
Which brings up the most important idea here.

51
00:02:21,926 --> 00:02:24,062
The database is always on the server side.

52
00:02:24,329 --> 00:02:25,107
Always.

53
00:02:25,676 --> 00:02:28,044
Your phone only ever holds a temporary copy.

54
00:02:28,613 --> 00:02:30,900
Just what's on the screen this very second.

55
00:02:32,328 --> 00:02:34,789
The real, permanent data lives on the server.

56
00:02:35,277 --> 00:02:39,096
Pull to refresh, and the truth comes straight from the kitchen.

57
00:02:40,153 --> 00:02:43,659
Your phone is a window onto the data, not where it's kept.

58
00:02:46,000 --> 00:02:48,786
And that leads to the golden rule of system design.

59
00:02:49,204 --> 00:02:50,052
Say it with me.

60
00:02:50,354 --> 00:02:51,631
Never trust the client.

61
00:02:52,293 --> 00:02:52,722
Why?

62
00:02:53,488 --> 00:02:55,416
Because anyone can mess with their own phone.

63
00:02:56,310 --> 00:03:00,118
Picture a hacked app that claims this order costs one rupee.

64
00:03:01,174 --> 00:03:02,951
The server doesn't just believe it.

65
00:03:03,369 --> 00:03:05,261
It re-checks against its own data.

66
00:03:05,830 --> 00:03:07,583
The real price is two ninety-nine.

67
00:03:08,152 --> 00:03:11,635
Even if you tampered with the app, the kitchen has the final say.

68
00:03:12,123 --> 00:03:12,889
Always.

69
00:03:15,000 --> 00:03:17,589
So how do the dining room and the kitchen actually talk?

70
00:03:17,891 --> 00:03:18,796
Through a waiter.

71
00:03:19,458 --> 00:03:21,641
In tech, we call that waiter the API.

72
00:03:22,303 --> 00:03:23,603
And here's the neat part.

73
00:03:24,021 --> 00:03:26,320
The same kitchen can serve many dining rooms.

74
00:03:26,680 --> 00:03:29,489
The iOS app, the Android app, the website.

75
00:03:30,151 --> 00:03:34,981
All different clients, all talking to one single backend, through that same waiter.

76
00:03:35,747 --> 00:03:36,966
Why build it this way?

77
00:03:37,535 --> 00:03:38,429
Three reasons.

78
00:03:38,789 --> 00:03:41,691
Security, so the rules live in one trusted place.

79
00:03:42,748 --> 00:03:45,221
Reuse, one backend for every app.

80
00:03:45,987 --> 00:03:48,344
And scale, so you can grow each side on its own.

81
00:03:50,000 --> 00:03:51,741
Quick question, but an important one.

82
00:03:52,229 --> 00:03:53,936
Who actually draws the page you see?

83
00:03:54,598 --> 00:03:55,782
There are two answers.

84
00:03:56,351 --> 00:03:57,593
Server-side rendering.

85
00:03:58,359 --> 00:04:01,111
The kitchen plates the whole dish and sends it out ready.

86
00:04:01,680 --> 00:04:04,490
The page shows up fast, and search engines love it.

87
00:04:04,850 --> 00:04:07,601
The catch: more work for the server every time.

88
00:04:08,263 --> 00:04:09,505
Client-side rendering.

89
00:04:09,737 --> 00:04:14,718
The kitchen sends raw ingredients and a recipe, and your phone cooks the page itself.

90
00:04:15,206 --> 00:04:18,886
Slower to show that first screen, but buttery smooth after.

91
00:04:19,374 --> 00:04:20,872
Most big apps do both.

92
00:04:21,232 --> 00:04:24,459
First screen from the server, everything after on the phone.

93
00:04:24,877 --> 00:04:26,270
A trade-off, not a winner.

94
00:04:28,000 --> 00:04:29,091
Okay, question two.

95
00:04:29,509 --> 00:04:31,111
Your phone wants Swiggy's kitchen.

96
00:04:31,599 --> 00:04:33,608
But there are billions of computers out there.

97
00:04:34,026 --> 00:04:35,605
And all your phone has is a name.

98
00:04:36,371 --> 00:04:37,346
swiggy dot com.

99
00:04:37,764 --> 00:04:39,389
A name is easy for you to remember.

100
00:04:39,958 --> 00:04:42,141
But the network doesn't move data to names.

101
00:04:42,803 --> 00:04:44,335
It moves data to numbers.

102
00:04:44,823 --> 00:04:46,762
So we have a little translation problem.

103
00:04:49,000 --> 00:04:51,450
Because every server on the internet has a number.

104
00:04:51,937 --> 00:04:53,342
It's called an IP address.

105
00:04:53,644 --> 00:04:55,095
Swiggy's might look like this.

106
00:04:55,664 --> 00:04:57,998
One-four-two, dot two-fifty, and so on.

107
00:04:58,567 --> 00:05:00,656
Think of it as the server's phone number.

108
00:05:01,423 --> 00:05:03,571
This shape is called IPv4.

109
00:05:04,058 --> 00:05:06,392
The newer IPv6 ones are much longer.

110
00:05:07,054 --> 00:05:11,431
But the idea is the same: a number that points to one exact machine.

111
00:05:13,000 --> 00:05:15,125
So how do we get from the name to the number?

112
00:05:15,694 --> 00:05:17,087
With the internet's phonebook.

113
00:05:17,505 --> 00:05:18,875
It's called DNS.

114
00:05:19,444 --> 00:05:23,170
You type swiggy dot com, and DNS finds the matching number.

115
00:05:23,658 --> 00:05:26,700
Just like tapping a saved contact and letting your phone dial it.

116
00:05:27,362 --> 00:05:28,383
You remember the name.

117
00:05:29,045 --> 00:05:30,485
DNS remembers the number.

118
00:05:30,903 --> 00:05:32,040
You never have to.

119
00:05:34,000 --> 00:05:36,229
But that lookup isn't one quick step.

120
00:05:36,647 --> 00:05:40,211
It's more like a relay race, handing a question down the line.

121
00:05:40,873 --> 00:05:42,231
Your device checks first.

122
00:05:42,649 --> 00:05:44,101
Do I already have this saved?

123
00:05:44,762 --> 00:05:48,976
If not, it asks a helper called the resolver, who does the running around.

124
00:05:49,394 --> 00:05:55,316
The resolver asks a root server, who says, I don't know, but ask the dot-com people.

125
00:05:56,372 --> 00:05:59,263
The dot-com server says, ask Swiggy's own server.

126
00:05:59,751 --> 00:06:03,408
And that last one, the authoritative server, finally knows the number.

127
00:06:03,710 --> 00:06:05,254
It races all the way back.

128
00:06:05,916 --> 00:06:07,878
Root and dot-com never knew the address.

129
00:06:08,365 --> 00:06:10,409
They just point you to whoever's next.

130
00:06:12,000 --> 00:06:14,264
Now, do you run that whole race every single time?

131
00:06:14,752 --> 00:06:15,170
No.

132
00:06:15,738 --> 00:06:17,074
The first time, yes.

133
00:06:17,561 --> 00:06:20,313
After that, your device just remembers the number.

134
00:06:20,731 --> 00:06:22,937
So the second visit is basically instant.

135
00:06:24,167 --> 00:06:26,327
How long should it remember before checking again?

136
00:06:26,814 --> 00:06:29,914
That's a setting called TTL, and it's a trade-off.

137
00:06:30,274 --> 00:06:34,964
Hold it a long time and you save trips, but you're slow to notice if the number changed.

138
00:06:35,626 --> 00:06:38,563
Short, and you're always fresh but doing more work.

139
00:06:39,132 --> 00:06:43,010
Oh, and one myth to kill: DNS only finds the address.

140
00:06:43,579 --> 00:06:45,483
It never loads the actual page.

141
00:06:47,000 --> 00:06:47,441
Right.

142
00:06:47,534 --> 00:06:48,533
We found the kitchen.

143
00:06:49,194 --> 00:06:51,853
Now the dining room and the kitchen need a shared language.

144
00:06:52,271 --> 00:06:53,861
That language is HTTP.

145
00:06:54,279 --> 00:06:55,684
Your phone sends a request.

146
00:06:56,172 --> 00:06:57,368
Get me the restaurants.

147
00:06:58,262 --> 00:07:00,003
The server sends back a response.

148
00:07:00,421 --> 00:07:02,035
Two hundred, OK, here they are.

149
00:07:02,453 --> 00:07:04,206
One ask, one answer.

150
00:07:04,775 --> 00:07:06,540
And then it forgets you completely.

151
00:07:06,899 --> 00:07:09,338
HTTP keeps no memory between requests.

152
00:07:09,825 --> 00:07:10,731
Hold that thought.

153
00:07:10,963 --> 00:07:12,217
It matters in a minute.

154
00:07:14,000 --> 00:07:15,463
Let's open up that request.

155
00:07:15,660 --> 00:07:16,485
Four parts.

156
00:07:16,972 --> 00:07:19,283
The method, the verb, what you want done.

157
00:07:19,700 --> 00:07:21,651
The path, which thing you want.

158
00:07:22,139 --> 00:07:24,530
The headers, little labels like who you are.

159
00:07:25,018 --> 00:07:27,282
And the body, any data you're sending up.

160
00:07:27,700 --> 00:07:30,672
And the address itself, the URL, packs a lot in.

161
00:07:31,241 --> 00:07:33,435
The scheme, how to talk, https.

162
00:07:34,004 --> 00:07:35,757
The host, which server.

163
00:07:36,326 --> 00:07:37,522
The path, which page.

164
00:07:37,940 --> 00:07:39,554
And the query, extra options.

165
00:07:39,972 --> 00:07:41,493
Four colours, one line.

166
00:07:43,000 --> 00:07:45,264
Of all those methods, two do most of the work.

167
00:07:45,926 --> 00:07:47,005
GET and POST.

168
00:07:47,423 --> 00:07:48,573
GET is for reading.

169
00:07:49,060 --> 00:07:50,024
Browsing the menu.

170
00:07:50,512 --> 00:07:54,169
It only looks, it changes nothing, and it's safe to do again and again.

171
00:07:55,225 --> 00:07:58,291
Its details ride right in the URL, so you can bookmark it.

172
00:07:58,709 --> 00:07:59,765
POST is for writing.

173
00:08:00,183 --> 00:08:01,275
Placing the order.

174
00:08:01,843 --> 00:08:07,486
It changes something on the server, and its data rides hidden in the body, not the URL.

175
00:08:08,055 --> 00:08:08,763
One warning.

176
00:08:09,529 --> 00:08:12,803
Send a POST twice, and you might place two orders.

177
00:08:13,465 --> 00:08:16,251
We'll fix that double-tap problem in a later episode.

178
00:08:18,000 --> 00:08:20,775
Remember how HTTP forgets you after every request?

179
00:08:21,344 --> 00:08:23,213
So how does the app keep you logged in?

180
00:08:23,875 --> 00:08:24,653
With a cookie.

181
00:08:25,221 --> 00:08:26,754
Think of it as an ID badge.

182
00:08:27,114 --> 00:08:28,101
You log in once.

183
00:08:28,519 --> 00:08:30,759
The server hands your browser a little token.

184
00:08:31,526 --> 00:08:32,431
Set-Cookie.

185
00:08:33,093 --> 00:08:36,425
From then on, every request you send quietly carries that cookie.

186
00:08:36,843 --> 00:08:38,991
And the server goes, ah, it's you again.

187
00:08:39,409 --> 00:08:40,280
Still logged in.

188
00:08:40,547 --> 00:08:42,056
That's all a session cookie is.

189
00:08:42,323 --> 00:08:43,914
A badge that says it's me.

190
00:08:46,000 --> 00:08:49,611
Every response also comes stamped with a status code.

191
00:08:50,272 --> 00:08:52,420
A quick number telling you how it went.

192
00:08:53,187 --> 00:08:54,626
Two hundred is green.

193
00:08:55,114 --> 00:08:56,820
OK, here's what you asked for.

194
00:08:57,238 --> 00:08:58,817
Three-oh-one is a redirect.

195
00:08:59,119 --> 00:09:00,826
It moved, follow the arrow.

196
00:09:01,395 --> 00:09:02,347
Four-oh-four.

197
00:09:02,544 --> 00:09:03,310
Not found.

198
00:09:03,798 --> 00:09:06,700
That page doesn't exist, and that one's on your side.

199
00:09:07,362 --> 00:09:08,187
Five hundred.

200
00:09:08,546 --> 00:09:09,510
Server error.

201
00:09:09,812 --> 00:09:11,252
The kitchen itself broke.

202
00:09:11,913 --> 00:09:16,836
Easy way to remember: four-something is your fault, five-something is the server's.

203
00:09:19,000 --> 00:09:19,789
One more letter.

204
00:09:20,277 --> 00:09:22,042
The S in HTTPS.

205
00:09:22,611 --> 00:09:24,375
Plain HTTP is like a postcard.

206
00:09:24,863 --> 00:09:26,872
Anyone who handles it on the way can read it.

207
00:09:27,440 --> 00:09:29,414
Your card number, right there in the open.

208
00:09:30,471 --> 00:09:32,317
HTTPS is a sealed envelope.

209
00:09:32,886 --> 00:09:36,995
Your phone and the server quietly agree on a secret, then scramble everything.

210
00:09:37,564 --> 00:09:39,468
To anyone in between, it's gibberish.

211
00:09:39,770 --> 00:09:40,966
That's the little padlock.

212
00:09:41,860 --> 00:09:42,557
But careful.

213
00:09:43,044 --> 00:09:45,088
The padlock only means the line is encrypted.

214
00:09:45,575 --> 00:09:47,352
It does not mean the site is honest.

215
00:09:47,770 --> 00:09:49,883
Even scam sites can show a padlock.

216
00:09:52,000 --> 00:09:54,218
Let's run the whole thing, start to finish.

217
00:09:55,274 --> 00:09:56,969
You tap a restaurant on Swiggy.

218
00:09:57,457 --> 00:09:59,465
First, DNS finds the server's number.

219
00:09:59,953 --> 00:10:07,801
Then your phone sends a sealed HTTPS request to that server, which checks the rules and pulls the real data from its database.

220
00:10:08,370 --> 00:10:11,249
The answer travels back, and the food appears on your screen.

221
00:10:11,667 --> 00:10:15,754
Find the address, knock politely in the right language, get your answer.

222
00:10:16,416 --> 00:10:18,053
That, honestly, is the whole web.

223
00:10:20,000 --> 00:10:20,731
And that's it.

224
00:10:21,091 --> 00:10:23,495
You now know what's happening under every single tap.

225
00:10:23,982 --> 00:10:25,317
A client and a server.

226
00:10:25,735 --> 00:10:27,245
The dining room and the kitchen.

227
00:10:27,814 --> 00:10:29,067
Never trust the client.

228
00:10:29,555 --> 00:10:33,015
DNS, the phonebook, turning names into numbers.

229
00:10:33,317 --> 00:10:36,939
HTTP, the shared language of request and response.

230
00:10:37,426 --> 00:10:39,655
And HTTPS, the sealed envelope.

231
00:10:40,073 --> 00:10:40,747
But notice.

232
00:10:40,816 --> 00:10:42,001
All of this took time.

233
00:10:42,488 --> 00:10:46,250
The request travelled, the relay raced, the data came back.

234
00:10:46,668 --> 00:10:48,200
So how do we measure fast?

235
00:10:48,560 --> 00:10:51,823
That's latency versus throughput, and that's next time.

236
00:10:52,183 --> 00:10:53,019
See you there.
